{
  "id": "Compound::DB00412 - Gene::1557",
  "verbalization": "Rosiglitazone, Pioglitazone, and Thiazolidinediones are all linked through a shared biological pathway involving peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors (PPARs). The evidence suggests that Rosiglitazone, as a PPAR gamma agonist, is included in the broader category of thiazolidinediones, which are known to modulate gene expression by activating PPAR gamma. This activation leads to the upregulation of various genes, including those involved in drug metabolism. The binding of Pioglitazone to Cyp2c19, a cytochrome P450 enzyme, is a recurring mechanism across the paths, indicating a potential role in drug metabolism. The lowest common ancestors of Rosiglitazone and Pioglitazone point to the thiazolidinedione antidiabetic agent class, reinforcing the link between these compounds and their shared biological function. The integration of these pathways suggests that Rosiglitazone may influence Cyp2c19 expression through PPAR gamma activation, thereby affecting drug metabolism. The evidence supports a mechanism where PPAR gamma activation leads to the upregulation of Cyp2c19, potentially altering the metabolic profile of thiazolidinediones and other compounds in the class.",
